Check indexed extensional proofs with Eqlog

Allow dependent relation signatures to refer to members of earlier model
arguments, so ambient rules can relate distinct context models without
flattening their members. Preserve owner bindings in native and dynamic
evaluators, including foreign members of model relations.

Add eqlog-ett with a Lean-like frontend and an Eqlog kernel for context
extension, partial substitutions, identity types with equality
reflection, a small universe, and indexed inductive families with
dependent constructor telescopes and elimination. Morphisms follow
substitution. Fresh statement scopes keep equality local; open
definitions do not allocate images of hidden body operands.

Allocate three constructor expansion steps per elaborated call and check
the module with one close call. Keep call provenance separate from term
equality so a recursive result cannot restart its expansion budget.

Develop addition and multiplication laws and length-indexed vectors,
including append, replication, mapping, and sums. Cover rejected proofs,
invalid indices and universe codes, context isolation, partial images,
and the computation bound in evaluator integration tests.

Make native model evaluation incremental through transport checkpoints,
partial-map deltas, and changed-owner frontiers. Reuse graph topology
and fully prepared primary relations when building alternate indices.
Keep inherited facts through equality quotients while preserving shared
subtrees, so unrelated changes do not replay existing transport.

Reconsider join priorities after each bound variable and connect
conflicting function results to one old-preferred representative.
Saturate functionality before larger joins while retaining independent
consumer checkpoints. Avoid repeated mapping-path and singleton iterator
allocations. Include runtime artifacts and compiler settings in the
component cache key.

Retain owner-scoped rule consumers through ambient feedback cycles, with
outer retries for equalities affecting another owner. Reuse prepared
primary slices for local alternate indices and skip transport unchanged
since traversal entry. Narrow equality rewrites using dependent
ownership and historical endpoint conflicts; imported models retain full
rewriting. Reuse SCC partitions when edge updates preserve their
topology. Preserve shared old-input subtrees and batch duplicate rule
outputs.

Saturate recursive global groups with functionality barriers and run
local rules during leaf-model transport. Stage transport frontiers until
the traversal finishes so interrupted closure retains unfinished work.
Share identical rule-join prefixes and group functionality lookups by
domain. Keep singleton difference cursors inline.
Prefer bound join keys after cheap function lookups, and avoid mapping
work in ordinary tree iteration.
Keep singleton suffix sets inline and intersect persistent snapshots
directly, preserving unchanged subtrees without temporary differences.
Expose unary suffix iteration and membership to generated join loops
across component crate boundaries. Filter historical iterator bindings
against canonical roots instead of rebuilding old-input intersections.
Use available membership guards to probe a smaller join input, retaining
original traversal for delta seeds and complete function-result lookups.
Update tuple suffixes during one tree descent and bypass mapping state
for ordinary lookups. Retain unchanged difference subtrees, compare
ordinary snapshots without allocating cursor stacks, and remember equal
publication roots. Reject empty relation roots before restricted joins.

Preserve the complete Nat and Vec development, bounded computation, and
one-call closure. Independent reviews confirm the mechanisms are general
Eqlog optimizations. Cover nested transport, partial maps, cross-owner
equality, dynamic imports, and interrupted closure with evaluator tests.
The latest full runs check all 52 declarations in 13.6 to 15.4 seconds
end to end. Further work is needed for the five-second target.
